  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper is present. Some figures and table layout are flattened in the supplied text, but the relevant Table 1 entries are identifiable.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper experiments on textual-sequence memorization (verbatim continuation), which it distinguishes from abstract knowledge unlearning. It reports no recovery attack after unlearning, so its experiments do not test the claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The study evaluates textual-sequence memorization erasure on the Training Data Extraction Challenge, not recovery of targeted knowledge by fine-tuning, activation edits, quantization, or prompting. It reports gradient-ascent results on sequence-memorization and utility metrics; these are recorded as related findings under fits_no_version. Its comparisons and discussion of recovery attacks do not report a recovery test.",
    "test_reason": "The paper studies erasure of verbatim textual-sequence memorization on GPT-Neo using the Training Data Extraction Challenge. It explicitly separates this from knowledge unlearning (WMDP, TOFU). It measures memorization and utility right after erasure. It runs no recovery attack: no relearning, activation ablation, quantization or prompting. It never measures targeted-knowledge accuracy against chance. Its results therefore bear on no claim version; they are related findings on a different construct.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that EMSO (entropy maximization with contrastive-gradient block selection) gives the best erasure-utility trade-off for large-scale textual-sequence memorization erasure. They report that NLL-based methods (GA, GD, KL) collapse and that erasure gets harder as models scale. They acknowledge that some requests are not fully erased.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper's proposed method is entropy maximization with selective weight updates; it also evaluates gradient ascent for textual-sequence memorization erasure. The task and evaluation are TSM erasure, not targeted-knowledge recovery.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Model scale changes TSM erasure effectiveness; the authors report that all methods are less effective on larger models. For GA, the reported language-generation metrics indicate collapse on all three tested model sizes.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "GA drives EL3 and MA to 0 on all three GPT-Neo sizes but collapses generation into text degeneration: Rep2 about 0.99, MAUVE about 0.01. GD and KL also collapse.",
        "proposed_claim": "On large-scale verbatim-memorization erasure, NLL-based unlearning (GA, GD, KL) removes measured memorization only by destroying generation utility.",
